Subject: [PATCH 08582/11362] [outer-loop] Phase 4c: naive change-magnitude cap
 does not fix Mode B (negative result)

REQ-ARC-WMTE-6240 (openspec/capabilities/arc-world-model-trust-energy/spec.md).

The 2026-07-29 admission-bottleneck note split 33 induced-engine rows
into Mode A (predicts no dynamics) and Mode B (over-writes 3x-10x too
many cells) and named a change-magnitude sparsity constraint on
predicted writes as cheap to test. This tests it.

Re-derived the same 21 Mode B cells (7 games x 3 seeds) from the same
frozen engine fixtures (results/arc_e3_origin_fixtures, confirmed
still on disk and untouched) the 2026-07-29 note used. Capped each
engine's per-transition write count at the largest change-count its
own shown (prefix) portion exhibited; discarded any held-out
prediction exceeding the cap.

The raw counts looked like a win (13/21 cells improve their
spurious-to-correct ratio, mean 102.1 -> 5.8) and were not: an honest
per-cell classification shows 11/21 cells collapse to a PURE NO-OP
(correct AND spurious both hit exactly 0 -- Mode B turned into Mode A,
not fixed), 8/21 are inert (the cap never fired), and only 2/21 show
the intended trim-excess-keep-genuine effect. Both of the plan's
stated raw-count gates are satisfied identically by a genuine fix and
by a degenerate collapse, which is exactly why the naive reading
looked better than the mechanism actually is.

Retires the specific naive hard-cap construction (a single threshold
with zero partial credit is too brittle once later-episode
transitions are genuinely larger than the shown prefix's). Does not
retire the general idea that Mode B is constrainable -- a graded
penalty rather than a hard cutoff was not tested and remains open. No
code shipped; analysis only. adversarial_verify.py and
determination_preservation_lint.py both clean.

Committed with --only: extensive concurrent conductor activity
(capstone spec, several arc_e3 engine files, an unrelated exp6224)
was in the working tree at commit time.

